A||||3||Rrazzz||Rusty spark plugs||||9||
Z||000000||Rrazzz||06-30-2000||10:15 PM||||The number 1 and number 2 spark plugs (i.e. passenger side) on my 1888 4 cylinder keep getting rusty. Sometimes I even find a puddle of water around the spark plug area in the head. This can't be good for getting a nice spark. It appears to be rain leaking into the engine compartment from between the right side engine grill and the right side edge of the engine lid.<P>Does anyone else have this problem?<BR>I've been thinking of building a rain deflector and installing it above the spark plug pockets. But its crowded; need to avoid the spark plugs/wires, the hood tension rod, battery cable, etc. <BR>Has anyone found a solution to prevent wet/rusty spark plugs.||||reg||9
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||06-30-2000||10:52 PM||||That water shouldn't make much of a difference to the spark. Spark plugs should be air tight so that the preasure in the combustion chamber pushes the cylinder down, not blow out the spark plug hole, altho that water/rust make make it a major job to get those plugs out in the future.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bruce||06-30-2000||11:02 PM||||From what I understand, the weather stripping that would have protected the plugs from rain and moisture was removed as part of a recall.||||reg||1
Z||000003||webbee||07-01-2000||02:56 AM||||If you use anti-seize compound you will never have trouble getting the plugs out. You only have to put about a half turn on them after they bottom. You should just have to bump the ratchett to get them to break.||||reg||1
